WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.451
Like its predecessor, the long-awaited Frozen
2 is ostensibly a movie for children, but

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.080
lots of grown-ups are watching it.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.800
If anything, Frozen 2 doubles down on bits
that seem aimed squarely at adults.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.380
Here's a look at just a few of the most memorable.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.260
The first Frozen film received a fair amount
of criticism for its lack of diversity.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.420
The movie's main cast and most, if not all,
background characters were white.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:28.380
Defenders of the film cited the apparent setting
of medieval Scandinavia as the reason for

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:33.000
the whiteness, but that excuse didn't really
hold water, as the film's setting of Arendelle

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.300
is a fictional kingdom and Scandinavia is
never mentioned.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.880
But Disney was clearly paying attention to
the controversy, as Frozen 2 is certainly

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:41.780
more diverse.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.600
For starters, there is now a prominent black
character.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.600
Lt. Mattias, voiced by Sterling K. Brown,
is an Arendellian soldier who shows up early

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.060
in the film and goes on to appear pretty consistently
throughout.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:58.140
Then there are the Northuldra, a clan of indigenous
people who live in an enchanted forest and

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.320
are pretty central to Frozen 2's story.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.040
They're based on the real-life Sámi people
from Scandinavia, and Disney worked with Sámi

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.880
groups in developing these characters.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.640
Kids aren't likely to notice the effort that
went into increasing Frozen 2's diversity,

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:12.640
but adults will.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:18.040
Despite all the attention Elsa's sexuality
receives from fans, only Anna has ever shown

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:19.660
any interest in romance.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:23.600
In Frozen, she spends much of the film pining
after her suitor, Hans.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.890
Then, after he turns out to be the villain,
she falls for Kristoff.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.840
"I could kiss you!

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:30.840
I could.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:31.860
I mean, I'd like to.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:32.480
May I?

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:32.980
We me?

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:33.480
I mean…

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.120
May we?

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:34.860
Wait, what?"

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.040
"We may."

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.860
In Frozen 2, it's made clear from the get-go
that Anna is still very much interested in

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.980
men, or, well, one man in particular.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.200
Anna and Kristoff are very much a couple.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.360
They even casually kiss from time to time,
which is kind of an unusual thing to see in

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:51.760
a Disney animated movie.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:55.880
But one scene in particular makes it seem
like Anna has more than kissing on the brain.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:01.120
In the film, Anna, Kristoff, Elsa, and Olaf
head out on an adventure together in a wagon

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.740
pulled by Sven the reindeer.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.960
Kristoff and Anna ride together in the front
of the wagon, while Elsa and Olaf sit in the

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:07.460
back.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:12.320
At a certain point in the journey, both Olaf
and Elsa doze off, which doesn't go unnoticed

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:13.120
by Anna.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.440
She points the situation out to Kristoff and
suggestively asks him what he wants to do

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.980
before puckering up her lips.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:22.860
Kids will think Anna just wanted a smooch,
but adults know that a mere smooch doesn't

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.980
require privacy with the added danger of getting
caught.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:30.020
Anyone who's seen Frozen knows that Olaf is
full of corny jokes.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:34.700
Kids may love the snowman's sense of humor,
while to adults, he can get a little annoying.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.160
"Olaf, did Elsa build you?"

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:37.860
"Yeah.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:38.380
Why?"

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:39.440
"Do you know where she is?"

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:40.280
"Yeah.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:40.780
Why?"

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.140
"Do you think you could show us the way?"

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:42.740
"Yeah.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:43.240
Why?"

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:44.120
"How does this work?"

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:44.780
"Ow!"

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:49.640
But in Frozen 2, Olaf actually manages to
sneak in a couple one-liners that are aimed

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:53.100
squarely at the grown-ups, and one of them
is actually pretty morbid.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:56.000
There's an old aviation joke regarding black
boxes.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.860
So-called black boxes, if you're unaware,
are flight recorders that are designed to

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:03.980
survive a plane crash in order to help investigators
determine what caused it.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:07.980
The joke goes something like this: "If the
black box is crash-proof, then why don't they

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.940
make the whole plane out of the black box?"

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.220
Olaf tells a variation of this joke in the
movie, and he does so at the most inappropriate

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.000
moment.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.660
In the film, Elsa and Anna find the shipwreck
that killed their parents.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.540
an unspeakably tragic discovery.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.920
Upon finding it, one of them declares that
there should be a record on board in a waterproof

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.160
box, as that was standard procedure.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:31.860
Olaf then, instead of consoling his friends
in this extremely trying time, asks, quote,

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.980
"If it's waterproof, why didn't they make
the whole ship waterproof?"

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:38.819
Kids will laugh at the joke, not knowing that
it's a reference to a very old joke about

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.240
airplanes and that Olaf is being extremely
insensitive.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.620
Adults, meanwhile, may want to see Olaf melt
even more than they already did.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:51.840
One of the film's big reveals comes near the
end, when Elsa discovers why Arendelle and

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.040
the Northuldra had gone to war in the past.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.840
It all started because the King of Arendelle,
Elsa and Anna's grandfather, was a colonizer

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:03.380
who, upon discovering the Northuldra, secretly
formulated a plan to destroy them.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:03.880
Why?

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:07.380
Because they believe in, and essentially worship,
the magic of the forest.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:11.739
These beliefs differed from his own, which
frightened him, so he constructed a dam on

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:15.080
their river under the guise of it being a
gift, and destroyed their land.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:19.069
When called out on this by the Northuldra
leader, the king killed him, igniting the

00:04:19.069 --> 00:04:20.760
battle between the two people.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.200
Frozen 2's stance on a number of issues has
been clear to critics.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:29.200
The film presents a narrative that shows the
harm done by colonialism, religious bigotry,

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.780
and environmental recklessness.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.279
In the end, the dam must be destroyed to lift
the curse on the forest, symbolizing a return

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.420
to the natural order of things as well as
the destruction of a symbol of oppression.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.779
These are heavy themes, and since they're
not spelled out in a super obvious manner,

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.840
kids probably won't understand a whit of it.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.220
With any luck, their parents will explain
it to them.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:53.400
If there's one theme that best sums up Frozen
2, it's this: Love thyself.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:58.120
The movie sends Elsa on a journey of self-discovery
to find the meaning in her life she's been

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:01.320
searching for forever, and she ends up finding...herself.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.780
Near the start of the film, Elsa hears a strange
siren's song that no one else can hear.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:09.819
This eventually leads her to the enchanted
forest, where she learns that the four spirits

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:13.540
of the forest, air, wind, fire, and earth,
are in disarray.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:17.600
After she learns that she has the power to
tame the spirits, she's told of a mysterious

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.620
fifth spirit that acts as a bridge between
all the others.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:25.020
Elsa ventures to find this fifth spirit, the
one who's been calling to her since the start

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.940
of the film, and she ends up in a temple trying
to chase down the elusive voice.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:33.480
When she reaches the end of the temple, she
realizes that not only is she the fifth spirit,

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.020
but she is also the one she's been searching
for.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:40.200
The film's message of self-love is an important
one, no matter the viewer, and no matter their

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:40.700
age.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:45.520
Everyone needs to love and take care of themselves,
and everyone, even adults, can use a reminder

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.040
of that from time to time.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.860
Even if it's from a cartoon.

